//
// Normative rule H_cause_virtual_instruction_high (src/priv/hypervisor.adoc):
//   A special rule applies for CSR instructions that access 32-bit high-half CSRs such as cycleh
//     and htimedeltah. When V=1 and XLEN=32, an invalid attempt to access a high-half CSR raises a
//     virtual-instruction exception instead of an illegal-instruction exception if the same CSR
//     instruction for the corresponding low-half CSR (e.g.cycle or htimedelta) is HS-qualified.
//
// Context:
//   RV32 only (the file passes trivially on RV64). The rule compares with the same instruction on
//     the low half: a read of cycleh is HS-qualified when mcounteren.CY=1, a write of cycleh never
//     is (cycle is read-only), htimedeltah and hedelegh accesses are (htimedelta and hedeleg are HS
//     CSRs), and mstatush is not (mstatus is M-level). Counter-enable bits in mcounteren,
//     hcounteren and scounteren may be read-only zero; each case sets the requested bits, reads
//     them back and expects the outcome for the values actually held.
//
// Test plan:
//   1. RV32, VS-mode and VU-mode: htimedeltah and hedelegh reads and writes raise virtual-
//     instruction.
//   2. cycleh and instreth (Zicntr): with mcounteren=1, hcounteren=0 a read raises virtual-
//     instruction; with mcounteren=0 it raises illegal-instruction; a write (csrrw, all enables
//     set) raises illegal-instruction.
//   3. mstatush read in VS-mode raises illegal-instruction.
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 7571
//

